WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.549
Film Courage: You talked about being in television
and with some of the jobs [you held] the hours

00:00:03.549 --> 00:00:08.040
were so long that you really didn’t have
much of a creative life?

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.820
So can you talk about leaving that world and
going to do your own thing with the writing,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:14.820
your schedule, etc.?

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.860
Andrew Horng: Yeah, it was from one extreme
to the other.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.980
In one situation I’m coming home late at
night tired and trying to write, falling asleep

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.970
after like three sentences.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:33.930
And then all of the sudden, I have all these
hours, I’m writing, trying to work on my

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:40.120
process, trying to figure out exactly what
I need to do as a writer to get the best product

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.739
out there.

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:51.399
So I left the TV industry and I really wasn’t
sure what type of a writer I was.

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:58.630
I thought I was a writer who needed to focus
on creating those rich characters and developing

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.980
that and trying to explore.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:06.260
But I started to realize that I was a writer
that needed more organization or structure

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:12.280
and I started to learn more about the structure
of screenplays, scenes and everything like

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.280
that.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.300
Film Courage: How was that having, so much
free time?

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.450
Because sometimes having too much free time
is a curse because you don’t structure it

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.200
and just think “Oh, I can just do that tomorrow.”

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.830
And then it’s a week later.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:25.830
Andrew Horng: Right.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:32.450
You kind of expand your days and the days
kind of seem like one.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.210
What I would do is I’d go to place to write.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.049
I still do this, I’d go to the library.

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:42.760
That’s still probably my favorite place
to write.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.960
There are some other places to go, too.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:53.470
I heard the writer of THE STING actually wrote
his screenplay in an airport.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:02:01.159
He had the busy people shuffling back and
forth and he was able to do it.&nbsp;

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:07.340
I’ve also heard of other writers (it’s
kind of a little bit extreme) but that actually

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:15.510
checks into a hotel, removes all of his clothes
and gives them to a friend and says “I’ve

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:22.140
got to finish this screenplay or else I’m
going to be losing money and I’m totally

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.450
by myself without anything.”

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.860
At some point the maid is going to come in.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.700
That’s a little bit more extreme.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.950
Film Courage: Don’t try that at the library
by the way.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.310
Andrew Horng: Yeah, I’m probably not going
to try that at the library but…who knows?

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:37.310
Maybe?

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:46.870
I’ve heard of one writer who actually wrote
on a boat from the US to Asia (some sort of

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:52.440
boat) and he didn’t have any distractions
with cell phones or anything like that.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.560
He just needed some time and there was a deadline,
too.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:01.590
I’d say any type of deadline (at least for
me) really does help.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.190
But it’s got to be the right deadline.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:06.120
It can’t be any arbitrary deadline.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.420
You can’t just say ‘by tomorrow I’m
going to be done.’

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:17.209
Film Courage: Yeah and writing at the library
is great because you can’t always get Wi-Fi

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:21.379
unless you log into their system and agree
to their terms.

00:03:21.379 --> 00:03:23.000
So sometimes it’s nice to just say “You
know what?

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.580
I’m not going to have Wi-Fi in here.”

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:25.680
And you get stuff done.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.959
Andrew Horng: Sometimes Wi-Fi is your biggest
enemy.

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:34.690
I mean sometimes you can use it to research,
but sometimes you want to shy away from it.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:41.840
I actually got rid of my Internet so I have
to go to the gym in my complex to use the

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.870
Internet and it keeps me a little bit more
focused.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.800
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.849
And that was a conscious decision because
you knew it distracted you?

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:53.370
Andrew Horng: I didn’t want to be researching
all the time.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:58.849
Yeah it really puts me in the zone and I don’t
also only write at my place, I go out.

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:04.560
I do a lot of writing outside.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.390
Film Courage: Do people ever stop you and
ask what you’re doing?

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:14.890
Andrew Horng: Yeah…people are curious and
I give them a little pitch.&nbsp;

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:15.909
Film Courage: What’s your pitch?

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:19.039
Andrew Horng: Right now I am working on an
ensemble film.

00:04:19.039 --> 00:04:24.860
It’s a high school drama about bullying
and abuse and there are lots of different

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:34.460
characters and lots of storylines that intersect
and it’s kind of like the TRAFFIC of bullying

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.529
or CRASH of bullying (that issue).

00:04:38.529 --> 00:04:44.099
And it’s all set in one location (it’s
a school) and that’s what I’m working

00:04:44.099 --> 00:04:44.689
on for now.

